  "isLocked": true,
  "description": "Apply custom transformations to a pandas DataFrame column and append results to a new derived column.\n\nDataFrame.apply provides an expressive mechanism to execute arbitrary custom element-wise operations on tabular features.\n\n### Mathematical Formulation\n$$\n\\text{df}[\\text{new\\_col}] = \\text{df}[\\text{src\\_col}].\\operatorname{apply}(f)\n$$\n\n### Step-by-Step Implementation\n1. Select source column from DataFrame\n2. Apply custom vectorized transformation function\n3. Store mapped values into target derived column\n\n### Returns\nList of records representing the updated DataFrame with the new transformed column.",
  "theory": "## Understanding the Theory: Apply Custom Transforms\n\n### Core Intuition: Why Do We Need This?\nReal-world tabular datasets are frequently messy, containing missing values, duplicates, and unaligned columns. Pandas provides powerful DataFrame primitives for reshaping, filtering, and cleaning structured data before training machine learning models. **Apply Custom Transforms** performs structured data manipulation efficiently.\n\nDataFrame.apply provides an expressive mechanism to execute arbitrary custom element-wise operations on tabular features. In this problem, the goal is to: apply custom transformations to a pandas dataframe column and append results to a new derived column.\n\n### Mathematical Principles & Formulation\nThe core governing equation for this operation is:\n\n$$\n\\text{df}[\\text{new\\_col}] = \\text{df}[\\text{src\\_col}].\\operatorname{apply}(f)\n$$\n\nEvery term in this formulation plays a precise mathematical role in ensuring dimensional consistency, numerical stability, and correct algorithmic behavior.\n\n### Step-by-Step Computational Workflow\n1. **Phase 1**: Select source column from DataFrame\n2. **Phase 2**: Apply custom vectorized transformation function\n3. **Phase 3**: Store mapped values into target derived column\n\n### Practical Machine Learning Applications & Nuances\n* **Vectorized Array Operations**: Applying vectorized transformations operates on contiguous C memory buffers under the hood, running orders of magnitude faster than iterating with Python loops.\n* **Data Cleansing**: Handling duplicates and missing data prevents bias and data leakage into downstream machine learning models.\n* **Memory Management**: Choosing appropriate column dtypes (e.g. categorical or int32 instead of object) drastically reduces DataFrame memory usage.",
  "examples": [
    "Input: records = [{\"id\": 1, \"val\": 3.0}, {\"id\": 2, \"val\": 4.0}], src_col = \"val\", new_col = \"val_sq\", transform_type = \"square\"\n\nOutput: [{\"id\": 1, \"val\": 3.0, \"val_sq\": 9.0}, {\"id\": 2, \"val\": 4.0, \"val_sq\": 16.0}]\n\nExplanation: Evaluated pandas_apply_custom_functions under standard test conditions."
